      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's focus on the technical aspects of the /s/ sound. The constriction of the oral cavity, specifically the narrow gap formed between the tongue and the alveolar ridge, is crucial in shaping the acoustic properties of the /s/ sound. When air is forced through this constriction, it creates a turbulent airflow that results in a series of high-frequency noise bursts. These bursts are characterized by a rapid series of spectral peaks in the frequency domain, particularly in the higher registers where the sound is most pronounced.\n\nThe \"abrasive\" quality of the /s/ sound is directly related to the intensity and sharpness of these spectral peaks. The higher the frequency of the peaks, the more abrasive the sound will be perceived. This is because the human ear is particularly sensitive to high-frequency sounds, and the sharp, high-pitched nature of the /s/ sound can be quite startling or \"unsettling\" to the listener.\n\nIn contrast, the /z/ sound, which also involves a constriction of the oral cavity, has a more controlled airflow due to the tongue's position further back in the mouth. This results in a smoother, less turbulent sound with fewer and less intense spectral peaks in the higher registers",
